WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.859
Binary verdicts with critiques, not rating scales.

2
00:00:03.139 --> 00:00:07.320
The instinct is to ask for a score: rate helpfulness from 1 to 5.

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:13.125
Hamel Husain’s guide to LLM judges argues against this for most product work.

4
00:00:13.405 --> 00:00:20.765
Scores across many dimensions are hard to act on, evaluators read the scale differently (what separates a 3 from a 4?)

5
00:00:21.045 --> 00:00:27.211
, and in his experience a long list of averaged scores is often a sign of a weak eval process.

6
00:00:27.491 --> 00:00:41.165
His recommendation is a single pass or fail per criterion, decided by the person whose judgment defines quality (he calls them the principal domain expert), with a short written critique explaining each decision.

7
00:00:41.445 --> 00:00:54.203
Eugene Yan’s survey of judge research reaches a similar practical conclusion: prefer binary outputs where you can, because they tend to be judged more reliably and let you use classification metrics.

8
00:00:54.483 --> 00:01:04.723
Anthropic’s grading tips are a little more permissive (output only “correct” or “incorrect,” or a 1 to 5 score), but the shared principle is to be specific.

9
00:01:05.003 --> 00:01:10.571
Purely qualitative feedback can’t be aggregated, and an undefined scale can’t be calibrated.

10
00:01:10.851 --> 00:01:12.899
The critique earns its place three ways.

11
00:01:13.179 --> 00:01:17.189
It forces the judge to reason before deciding (more on that next).

12
00:01:17.469 --> 00:01:28.413
It’s the first thing you read when the judge disagrees with a person, so it turns “the judge is wrong 10 percent of the time” into “the judge doesn’t know rule 3 covers exchanges.

13
00:01:28.693 --> 00:01:33.045
” And good human critiques become the few-shot examples in the judge prompt.

14
00:01:33.325 --> 00:01:39.683
When something really is graded, such as a severity level, keep the levels few and define each one with examples.

15
00:01:39.963 --> 00:01:44.421
If you can’t write down what separates adjacent levels, the judge can’t either.
